lient\n   104|\n   105|---\n   106|\n   107|## 🔄 MEMORY & COMMUNICATIONS DATA FLOW\n   108|\n   109|### Pattern 1: Event Sourcing\n   110|```\n   111|┌─────────────┐\n   112|│  Application│\n   113|│  (Producer) │\n   114|└──────┬──────┘\n   115|       │ Emit Event\n   116|       ▼\n   117|┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n   118|│     PULSAR (Event Backbone)     │\n   119|│  ┌───────────────────────────┐  │\n   120|│  │   Topic: user-events      │  │\n   121|│  │   └─ Event Store          │  │\n   122|│  └───────────────────────────┘  │\n   123|│  ┌───────────────────────────┐  │\n   124|│  │   BookKeeper Storage      │  │\n   125|│  └───────────────────────────┘  │\n   126|└──────┬───────────────────────┘\n   127|       │ Subscribe\n   128|       ▼\n   129|┌────────────────┐\n   130|│  Consumers     │\n   131|│  - Query Store │  \n   132|│  - Analytics   │\n   133|│  - Search      │\n   134|└────────────────┘\n   135|```\n   136|\n   137|### Pattern 2: CDC (Change Data Capture)\n   138|```\n   139|┌─────────────┐\n   140|│ PostgreSQL  │──┐\n   141|│ MongoDB     │──┼──► Pulsar ──► ClickHouse ──► Grafana\n   142|│ Redis       │──┘    (CDC)     (Aggregate)    (Dashboards)\n   143|│ etc.        │\n   144|└─────────────┘\n   145|```\n   146|\n   147|### Pattern 3: Microservices Communication\n   148|```\n   149|Service A ──► NATS ──► Service B\n   150|             │\n   151|             ▼\n   152|        Service C\n   153|```\n   154|\n   155|---\n   156|\n   157|## 🎯 KEY ARCHITECTURAL DECISIONS\n   158|\n   159|### 1. Polyglot Persistence\n   160|**Why**: Use the right tool for the right job\n   161|- PostgreSQL for transactions\n   162|- MongoDB for documents\n   163|- ClickHouse for analytics\n   164|- Redis for caching\n   165|- Qdrant for vectors\n   166|\n   167|### 2. Event-Driven Architecture\n   168|**Why**: Decouple services, enable real-time processing\n   169|- Pulsar as event backbone\n   170|- NATS for lightweight messaging\n   171|- RedPanda for high-throughput streaming\n   172|\n   173|### 3. Schema Management via Prisma\n   174|**Why**: Type safety and database versioning\n   175|- Single source of truth: `/adaptai/dbops/prisma/schema.prisma`\n   176|- 60 models synchronized\n   177|- Controlled migrations\n   178|- Generated clients\n   179|\n   180|### 4. Port Standardization (18xxx)\n   181|**Why**: Security and organization\n   182|- 18000-18099: Custom services\n   183|- 180xx: Each database has dedicated port(s)\n   184|- Easy firewall rules\n   185|- Service discovery\n   186|\n   187|---\n   188|\n   189|## 📈 PERFORMANCE CHARACTERISTICS\n   190|\n   191|### Latency Comparison\n   192|```\n   193|NATS:      <1ms    (ultra-fast)\n   194|RedPanda:  ~3ms    (fast)\n   195|Pulsar:    ~5ms    (moderate)\n   196|Kafka:     ~10ms   (slower)\n   197|```\n   198|\n   199|### Throughput Comparison\n   200|```\n   201|RedPanda:  1M+ msgs/sec (highest)\n   202|ClickHouse: 100K+ queries/sec\n   203|Pulsar:    100K msgs/sec\n   204|NATS:      10K msgs/sec\n   205|```\n   206|\n   207|---\n   208|\n   209|## 🔌 INTEGRATION PATTERNS\n   210|\n   211|### PostgreSQL → Pulsar (CDC)\n   212|```javascript\n   213|// Debezium captures all changes\n   214|await producer.send({\n   215|  topic: 'db-changes',\n   216|  data: Buffer.from(JSON.stringify({\n   217|    op: 'INSERT',\n   218|    table: 'users',\n   219|    after: { id: 1, name: 'John' }\n   220|  }))\n   221|})\n   222|```\n   223|\n   224|### Pulsar → ClickHouse (Analytics)\n   225|```javascript\n   226|// Pulsar Function batches events\n   227|function processEvents(events) {\n   228|  // Batch and aggregate\n   229|  return aggregateData\n   230|}\n   231|```\n   232|\n   233|### Multiple Databases → Pulsar → Analytics\n   234|```\n   235|┌────────┐  ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐\n   236|│MongoDB │  │PostgreSQL│ │ Redis │\n   237|└───┬────┘  └───┬────┘  └───┬────┘\n   238|    │          │           │\n   239|    └──────────┼───────────┘\n   240|               ▼\n   241|