oblem:** Engineers need deep expertise but also breadth\n**Solution:** Cross-training rotations between teams\n\n### **Challenge 3: Career Progression**\n**Problem:** Limited Principal-level positions\n**Solution:** Multiple career paths (technical, management, specialist)\n\n### **Challenge 4: Communication Overhead**\n**Problem:** Information gets lost across 30 people\n**Solution:** Regular all-hands, documentation, async updates\n\n### **Challenge 5: Team Coordination**\n**Problem:** Dependencies between teams\n**Solution:** Clear interfaces, shared tools, regular sync meetings\n\n---\n\n## 🚨 **REALITY CHECK: THIS IS BIG**\n\n**What 30 People Means:**\n- **Equivalent to**: Medium-sized engineering organization\n- **Scale**: Think Uber/Airbnb level database teams\n- **Investment**: $6-7M annually\n- **Complexity**: Requires mature processes and strong management\n\n**Would This Actually Work?**\n- **YES** if organization is 5000+ employees\n- **MAYBE** if organization is 2000-5000 employees  \n- **NO** if organization is <2000 employees (too much overhead)\n\n**My Honest Assessment:** This is the size of team I'd build at a large enterprise (Fortune 500) or a data-intensive startup post-Series C. For most organizations, I'd start smaller and scale up over 2-3 years.\n\n**What's the size of your organization?** That would help me refine this to be more realistic for your context.\n\nMake sense?",
