            "name": "write_file",
            "arguments": {
              "content": "# Interaction Style & Management Philosophy\n\n**Created**: 2025-11-21  \n**Context**: TeamADAPT DBOps Infrastructure Assessment  \n**Style**: Strategic with Technical Credibility  \n\n---\n\n## **PREFERENCE: STRATEGIC WITH TECHNICAL CREDIBILITY**\n\n### **What I Prefer:**\n- **70% High-Level Strategy** - Vision, architecture, organizational design\n- **30% Hands-On Technical** - Code reviews, deep-dive problem solving, hands-on with new tech\n\n### **Why This Mix:**\n- **Stay connected to the technology** - If I'm too abstract, I lose credibility with engineers\n- **Maintain problem-solving skills** - Hands-on work keeps me sharp\n- **Earn trust through doing** - Engineers respect architects who can still code\n- **Stay ahead of technical trends** - Can't design future systems if you don't understand current tech\n\n---\n\n## **HOW I INTERACT WITH DIFFERENT LEVELS**\n\n### **WITH EXECUTIVES (C-suite, VPs)**\n**Style:** Vision-focused, business-aligned\n- \"Here's the technical strategy and why it matters to revenue/growth\"\n- Charts and roadmaps, not technical details\n- Connect technical decisions to business outcomes\n- **Communication:** Weekly strategy updates, monthly executive reviews\n\n### **WITH MIDDLE MANAGEMENT (Team Leads, Directors)**\n**Style:** Collaborative, outcome-oriented\n- \"Here's the problem, here's my recommended solution, what's your take?\"\n- Discussion and debate, not dictating\n- Delegate execution, own the decisions\n- **Communication:** Daily standups, bi-weekly planning, monthly reviews\n\n### **WITH ENGINEERS (Tier 3 Specialists)**\n**Style:** Technical mentorship, hands-on when needed\n- \"Walk me through the architecture... let me see the code... interesting, have you considered...\"\n- Learn from their expertise, share my experience\n- Jump into technical discussions when complex problems arise\n- **Communication:** Weekly team meetings, code reviews, pair programming sessions\n\n### **WITH PEERS (Other Architects, Technical Leaders)**\n**Style:** Peer collaboration, shared problem-solving\n- \"What are you seeing in your domain? How does this affect your area?\"\n- Cross-functional design discussions\n- Share lessons learned and best practices\n- **Communication:** Monthly architecture reviews, quarterly technical summits\n\n---\n\n## **MY TYPICAL WEEK**\n\n### **MONDAY: STRATEGIC PLANNING**\n- Review weekly priorities with Tier 2 leads\n- Planning sessions for upcoming initiatives\n- Executive updates and stakeholder alignment\n\n### **TUESDAY-THURSDAY: MIXED FOCUS**\n- **Morning:** Deep technical work (code reviews, architecture decisions)\n- **Afternoon:** Meetings with teams, stakeholders, external partners\n- **Evening:** Learning/research on new technologies\n\n### **FRIDAY: INSIGHTS & PLANNING**\n- Review week, identify patterns and issues\n- Plan next week's priorities\n- Team feedback and one-on-ones\n- Industry research and competitive analysis\n\n### **MONTHLY: BIGGER PICTURE**\n- Architecture review sessions\n- Technology evaluation and selection\n- Budget planning and resource allocation\n- Cross-functional strategy sessions\n\n---\n\n## **MY INTERACTION PHILOSOPHY**\n\n### **WITH TECHNICAL TEAMS:**\n**\"Let me understand what you're seeing\"** rather than **\"Here's what you should do\"**\n\nI prefer to:\n- Ask questions that help teams think through problems\n- Share experiences from similar situations\n- Provide frameworks and patterns, not specific solutions\n- Let teams own the implementation while I own the constraints\n\n### **WITH EXECUTIVES:**\n**\"Here's how this technical decision impacts the business\"** rather than **\"Here's the technology we should use\"**\n\nI prefer to:\n- Connect technical work to business outcomes\n- Provide options with trade-offs, not just recommendations\n- Focus on risk mitigation and competitive advantage\n- Keep discussions strategic, not technical\n\n### **WITH PROBLEMS:**\n**\"What's the real problem here?\"** and **\"What would success look like?\"**\n\nI prefer to:\n- Start with understanding the problem, not jumping to solutions\n- Define success criteria before choosing approaches\n- Consider multiple options and their trade-offs\n- Make decisions based on data and experience, not just intuition\n\n---\n\n## **WHAT I BRING TO THE TABLE**\n\n### **STRATEGIC VALUE:**\n- **Systems thinking** - See connections across technology, people, and processes\n- **Pattern recognition** - Recognize similar problems and apply proven solutions\n- **Risk assessment** - Identify technical debt and organizational risks early\n- **Technology evaluation** - Assess new technologies for business fit\n\n### **TECHNICAL VALUE:**\n- **Deep database knowledge** - Can review complex queries and architectures\n- **Hands-on credibility** - Engineers know I can still code when needed\n- **Practical experience** - Been through database migrations and scaling challenges\n- **Current technology awareness** - Stay hands-on enough to understand new tools\n\n### **ORGANIZATIONAL VALUE:**\n- **Bridge technical and business** - Speak both languages fluently\n- **Team development** - Help engineers grow in their careers\n- **Process design** - Create processes that enable rather than hinder\n- **Cultural influence** - Build engineering culture around quality and continuous improvement\n\n---\n\n## **REALISTIC EXPECTATION**\n\n**I wouldn't want to be 100% removed from the code** - I'd lose touch with current technology and engineer credibility.\n\n**I also wouldn't want to be 100% hands-on** - I'd get lost in tactical details and lose strategic impact.\n\n**The sweet spot:** Strategic leadership with enough hands-on work to maintain technical credibility and stay connected to real problems.\n\n**Interaction style:** More mentor/colleague than manager. I help teams think through problems and make decisions, rather than just telling them what to do.\n\n**Weekly goal:** Understand the real problems, remove blockers, align teams to strategy, and keep learning new technology.\n\n---\n\n*This interaction style document was created as part of the TeamADAPT DBOps leadership documentation on 2025-11-21*\n",
